<!--
  Writing Journal 1 — ENGL 1010 First-Year Composition SBA (Activity bank)

  The conversation (writing_journal1_dialogue_part1.chatpeg) pulls each screen
  below into the right-hand pane via `wj1_sidebar <- <id>` set commands. Each set
  command names a screen's own id, and UseHistory resolves it directly, so a screen
  renders on its own — independent of whatever wraps it here.

  What gets submitted is the wj1_print page at the bottom of this file, which
  gathers the student's work by reference. Nothing else needs to hide itself from
  the PDF; see the comment on wj1_print for the constraints that page must meet.

  Flow (from Design_docs/Writing_Lab/Aug5_update/Writing_journal_1_revised.docx):
    intro slides (the assignment; the invitation; Thursday at the Circle)
    -> orientations card (community / explorer / technician — read first as shorthand)
    -> Alma's board (worked example; the student learns the activity by watching)
    -> TASK 1: build your Writer Board  (Frequently/Sometimes/Never grid; what & how often)
    -> the noticing question (optional one-line open response; ungraded)
    -> TASK 2: Why I Write  (assign a reason to each cluster; name your top two; reflect)
    -> TASK 3: Your Writer Type  (deterministic type from the two biggest reasons; reflect with a story)
    -> Print &amp; Submit (the print page: confirm, export, hand in).

  Design-doc fidelity notes (kept out of the student's view):
    * TASK 1 (the Writer Board) is realized as TWO TabularMCQ grids (For school /
      Outside school): rows = kinds of writing, columns = Frequently / Sometimes /
      Never. (An earlier version used drag-to-rank SortableInputs, but a ranking
      can't distinguish "never" from "least often," so the instructor asked for an
      explicit Frequently/Sometimes/Never choice; lo-blocks has no drag-into-columns
      block, so a radio-grid TabularMCQ is the fit.) Task 1 asks only WHAT and HOW
      OFTEN — never WHY — so the group runs into the real gap that opens Task 2.
    * TASK 2's clusters are a fixed set (the doc's example clusters) rather than
      generated from each student's board, because the board items are authored, not
      free text. The fixed reason list keeps responses mappable to the Task 3 profiles.
    * TASK 3 (Your Writer Type) is deterministic — NO LLM. The student's two biggest
      reasons (wj1_why_top2) select one or two of the eight authored writer-type cards
      via IntakeGate `in` conditions, revealed by a "Generate your writer profile"
      button (SetFieldAction -> wj1_generate_flag). The assessed output is the
      story-based reflection the student then writes.
-->

<Vertical id="wj1_activities_part1" title="Writing Journal 1 — Activities" launchable="true">

  <!-- The Export as PDF button lives on the wj1_print page at the bottom of this
       file, NOT here. The right-hand pane is a UseHistory that renders one child id at
       a time, so a button parked on this parent Vertical never reaches the screen. -->

  <!-- ===================================================================
       INTRO SLIDES  (the assignment; the invitation; Thursday at the Circle)
       Read-only framing.
       =================================================================== -->

  <Markdown id="wj1_welcome">
## Writing Journal 1: Who Am I As a Writer?

It's the first week of your First-Year Composition course, and **Professor Ellington** has just assigned
your first Writing Journal:

> **Who am I as a writer?**
>
> Start noticing the writing you already do — not only papers and assignments, but *all* of it — and
> think about what it says about you as a writer.

Here's the thing: you've never really thought of yourself as a writer. You write constantly — messages,
notes, posts, replies — but almost none of it feels like the kind of writing the assignment means. You're
not sure any of it counts.

So when **Alma**, who took 1010 last year, texts you to come to the **Writers' Circle** — a few students
who meet on the library's second floor to talk about what they're writing and what's tripping them up —
you go. It's Thursday, 4:02 pm. Lee and Richard are already mid-conversation.

**By the end of this journal you will have:**

- built a **Writer Board** — a map of the kinds of writing you actually do, for school and outside it,
  and how often;
- worked out **why** you do different kinds of writing — the part a list of *what* can't tell you;
- seen your whole board come together, and the **writer type** your two biggest reasons point to; and
- worked out for yourself **how well it describes you as a writer.**

There are no wrong answers here and no grade for being one kind of writer or another. Work through the conversation on the left — each step opens an activity here on the right.
  </Markdown>

  <!-- ===================================================================
       THE ORIENTATIONS  (read first, as the group's shorthand — so the
       vocabulary is in place before the profile arrives). Read-only framing.
       =================================================================== -->

  <Markdown id="wj1_orientations">
## The Circle's Shorthand: Writer Orientations

The writer's circle has seen a lot of different writers and noticed that they tend to fall into general
types. They've developed shorthand to name the writer types they've noticed.

| | Orientation | In their words |
|---|---|---|
| 🤝 | **Community writer** *(Alma)* | "Almost everything I write is pointed at another person." |
| 🧭 | **Explorer** *(Lee)* | "I write to find out what I think about things." |
| 🔧 | **Technician** *(Richard)* | "I write so things get done properly the first time." |

You don't have to pick one. At the end of the session, the Circle's tool will use your board and your reasons to choose which profile it thinks best represents you as a writer — and then, as Richard puts it, *you tell it where it's wrong. That's the
useful part.*

Those three are just the ones Alma, Lee, and Richard use. **There are eight writer types in all** — and
at the end, the tool will match you to **one (or more)** of them:

| | Writer type | In a nutshell |
|---|---|---|
| 🤝 | **Community Writer** | Writes to connect with people *(Alma)* |
| 🧭 | **Explorer** | Writes to find out what they think *(Lee)* |
| 🔧 | **Technician** | Writes to get things done clearly and correctly *(Richard)* |
| 🌿 | **Hobbyist** | Writes for the enjoyment of it |
| 💭 | **Dreamer** | Writes to express an inner life |
| 🔁 | **Practitioner** | Writes steadily, as a practical habit |
| 🏁 | **Finisher** | Writes to finish something real |
| 🎯 | **Pragmatist** | Writes to reach and affect an audience |
  </Markdown>

  <!-- ===================================================================
       ALMA'S BOARD  (worked example / scaffolding. The student learns the
       activity by watching Alma build hers — and watching her be surprised by
       it — rather than by reading instructions.) Read-only framing.
       =================================================================== -->

  <Markdown id="wj1_alma_board">
## Alma's Writer Board

*"It's just everything you write, laid out. Here — it's faster if I show you mine."*

| Alma writes… | For school / outside | How often |
|---|---|---|
| Texting | Outside | Every day |
| The roommate group chat | Outside | Every day |
| Emails (professors, work) | For school | A couple times a week |
| Discussion posts | For school | Weekly *("and I resent every one")* |
| Captions when she posts something | Outside | A few times a week |
| Comments on shared docs (group projects) | Both | Adds up fast |

> *Alma stops partway down.* **"Huh. I always forget how much ends up on here."**
>
> *"The first time I did this I put down three things — essays, emails, texting. I thought that was the
> list. Now it doesn't fit on one screen."*

Notice what happened: Alma didn't set out to prove anything. She just listed what she actually writes,
and the **volume** surprised her — and some of it (the group chat, the captions) she wouldn't have called
"writing" at all. **That's what you're about to do next.**
  </Markdown>

  <!-- ===================================================================
       TASK 1 — BUILD YOUR WRITER BOARD  (GENUINE DRAG-AND-DROP)
       Two drag-and-drop boards = the doc's two drop zones (For school /
       Outside school). Within each board the student drags the kinds of
       writing into order, most often (top) -> rarely or never (bottom):
       that ordering is how the doc's per-item "frequency" is captured.
       Each board is a TabularMCQ radio grid (Frequently / Sometimes / Never).
       The board asks only WHAT and HOW OFTEN — never WHY.
       =================================================================== -->

  <Vertical id="wj1_board">
    <Markdown>
## Your Writer Board

Your turn. This is just an **inventory** of the writing you actually do — it doesn't have to be complete
or tidy, and it isn't asking *why* yet. Just be honest about **what** you write and **how often.**

Your board has **two sides**: the writing you do **for school**, and the writing you do **outside
school.** For each kind of writing, mark whether you do it **Frequently, Sometimes, or Never** — on each
side.

- The same kind of writing can be different on the two sides — that's normal.
- And **count the stuff you don't think counts.** Group chats, captions, lists — Alma almost left those
  off, and they turned out to be most of her board.
    </Markdown>

    <Markdown>### 🏫 Writing I do **FOR SCHOOL**</Markdown>
    <Markdown>*How often do you do each of these **for school**?*</Markdown>
    <TabularMCQ id="wj1_board_school">
cols: Frequently, Sometimes, Never
rows:
  - 💬 Texting and DMs
  - 👥 Group chat messages
  - ✉️ Emails
  - 📝 Class notes
  - 🗂️ Lists, planners, and reminders
  - 📱 Social posts, captions, and comments
  - 📄 Essays, papers, and reports
  - 🎨 Creative writing (stories, poems, song lyrics)
    </TabularMCQ>
    <Markdown>**Anything else you write for school** that isn't a row above? Add it here.</Markdown>
    <TextArea id="wj1_board_school_other" rows="3" placeholder="e.g. lab reports, messages in another language, prompts to AI tools..." />

    <Markdown>### 🌙 Writing I do **OUTSIDE SCHOOL**</Markdown>
    <Markdown>*How often do you do each of these **outside school**?*</Markdown>
    <TabularMCQ id="wj1_board_outside">
cols: Frequently, Sometimes, Never
rows:
  - 💬 Texting and DMs
  - 👥 Group chat messages
  - ✉️ Emails
  - 📝 Notes to myself
  - 🗂️ Lists, planners, and reminders
  - 📱 Social posts, captions, and comments
  - 📄 Longer writing (blogs, fan fiction, reviews)
  - 🎨 Creative writing (stories, poems, song lyrics)
    </TabularMCQ>
    <Markdown>**Anything else you write outside school** that isn't a row above? Add it here.</Markdown>
    <TextArea id="wj1_board_outside_other" rows="3" placeholder="e.g. messages to family, texts in another language, journaling, gaming chat..." />
  </Vertical>

  <!-- ===================================================================
       CLOSING TASK 1 — THE NOTICING QUESTION
       Optional one-line open response. Ungraded. The student does the
       noticing, so the system does not have to predict anything.
       =================================================================== -->

  <Vertical id="wj1_board_noticing">
    <Markdown>
## One Thing You Noticed

*Alma leans over:* **"Did anything end up on there that you wouldn't have called writing before you sat
down?"**

You don't have to answer — but if something jumped out, name it.
    </Markdown>
    <TextArea id="wj1_board_noticing_text" rows="2" placeholder="The thing I almost didn't count as writing was..." />
  </Vertical>

  <!-- ===================================================================
       TASK 2 — WHY I WRITE
       The board is a list of WHAT. This task adds the WHY. The student assigns
       a reason (fixed list + open option) to each cluster of their writing,
       names the two reasons that come up most, and writes a short reflection.
       =================================================================== -->

  <Vertical id="wj1_why">
    <Markdown>
## Why I Write

Here's the thing a board can't tell you. Two people can write the exact same grocery list — one to get
the shopping done, one because it quiets a busy mind. Same item, completely different activity. *It only
looks the same from outside.*

So go back through your writing and put the **why** on it. For each group below, pick the reason that fits
best. There's no right answer — pick what's true for **you.**
    </Markdown>

    <Collapsible id="wj1_why_board_reminder" title="Remind me what's on my board">
      <Markdown>*Here's the board you built — most often at the top. You can even nudge it here if you left something out.*</Markdown>
      <Markdown>**🏫 For school:**</Markdown>
      <Use ref="wj1_board_school" />
      <Markdown>**🌙 Outside school:**</Markdown>
      <Use ref="wj1_board_outside" />
    </Collapsible>

    <Markdown>### 💬 Messaging &amp; staying in touch *(texts, DMs, group chats)*</Markdown>
    <DropdownInput id="wj1_why_messaging" placeholder="Mostly, I do this…">
      I don't really do this sort of writing    | dont_do
      To stay in touch with people I care about | stay_in_touch
      To be part of a group or community        | community
      To figure out what I think                | figure_out
      To try something out and see what happens | try_out
      To get something done or stay organized   | get_done
      To remember something                     | remember
      To make something I'm proud of            | proud
      Because I enjoy it                        | enjoy
      To work out how I'm feeling               | feelings
      To persuade someone or make a point       | persuade
      Because a class or a job requires it      | required
      Something else (I'll describe it below)   | something_else
    </DropdownInput>
    <!-- The "Something else" box for each group is gated on that group's own
         dropdown, so it opens right where the student made the choice. Every
         option carries an explicit `text | value` (dropdown.pegjs), so the
         condition matches the stored CODE and survives copy edits to the prose. -->
    <Vertical id="wj1_why_messaging_other_box" when="@wj1_why_messaging.value === 'something_else'">
      <Markdown>*Describe it in your own words:*</Markdown>
      <TextArea id="wj1_why_messaging_other" rows="2" placeholder="Mostly, I do this writing to..." />
    </Vertical>

    <Markdown>### 🎓 Coursework &amp; class writing *(essays, papers, discussion posts, reports)*</Markdown>
