    <Markdown>
### Your two biggest reasons for writing

Looking at the **whole board**, tick the **two** reasons that come up most often across everything you
write.
    </Markdown>
    <CheckboxInput id="wj1_why_top2">
      <Key value="stay_in_touch">To stay in touch with people I care about</Key>
      <Key value="community">To be part of a group or community</Key>
      <Key value="figure_out">To figure out what I think</Key>
      <Key value="try_out">To try something out and see what happens</Key>
      <Key value="get_done">To get something done or stay organized</Key>
      <Key value="remember">To remember something</Key>
      <Key value="proud">To make something I'm proud of</Key>
      <Key value="enjoy">Because I enjoy it</Key>
      <Key value="feelings">To work out how I'm feeling</Key>
      <Key value="persuade">To persuade someone or make a point</Key>
      <Key value="required">Because a class or a job requires it</Key>
      <Key value="something_else">Something else</Key>
    </CheckboxInput>

    <Markdown>
### One short reflection

A few honest sentences:

- Was there a reason you **expected** to pick and didn't?
- Or one that showed up **more than you thought** it would?
    </Markdown>
    <TextArea id="wj1_why_reflect" rows="6" placeholder="I expected to pick... but what actually came up most was... which surprised me because..." />
  </Vertical>

  <!-- ===================================================================
       TASK 3 — YOUR WRITER BOARD, ALL TOGETHER  (DETERMINISTIC; NO LLM)
       The culminating screen combines the student's Writer Board and their
       reasons into (1) a structured read-back of what/where/how-often + why
       (built straight from their own inputs via Ref), and (2) a writer-type
       card (or two) chosen DETERMINISTICALLY from the two biggest reasons the
       student named: each of the 8 authored profile cards is revealed by an
       IntakeGate gated on the relevant reason(s). No AI, no button, no LLM
       backend — the result is transparent, reproducible, and works in a
       server-free static export.

       Reason -> writer-type map (each reason triggers exactly one card):
         stay_in_touch, community  -> 🤝 Community Writer
         figure_out, try_out       -> 🧭 Explorer
         get_done, required        -> 🔧 Technician
         remember                  -> 🔁 Practitioner
         proud                     -> 🏁 Finisher
         enjoy                     -> 🌿 Hobbyist
         feelings                  -> 💭 Dreamer
         persuade                  -> 🎯 Pragmatist
       (something_else triggers no card.) Two reasons show one or two cards —
       the doc's "primary + a close second" without an opaque AI ranking.
       The reflection is refocused (per instructor) from "what did the guess
       get wrong" to "what does your board show about you as a writer?"
       =================================================================== -->

  <Vertical id="wj1_profile">
    <Markdown>
## Your Writer Type

You built your **Writer Board** and worked out **why** you do different kinds of writing. Based on the
**two biggest reasons** you named, the Circle's tool can suggest the writer type it thinks best represents
you — and if your two reasons pull in different directions, it will show you **more than one.** The Circle
uses eight of these — the same shorthand Alma, Lee, and Richard use. Remember Alma's rule:
it's a **shorthand, not a verdict.** You know things about yourself it can't.
    </Markdown>

    <!-- The tool only reads two reasons, so the button waits until exactly two
         are ticked back in Why I Write; the two nudges below say which way the
         count is off. The zero-reason fallback card further down stays as a
         second line of defense. -->
    <Markdown when="@wj1_why_top2.value.length &lt; 2">
*The tool works from **two** reasons. Pop back to **Why I Write**, tick the two that come up most across
your board, and this will be waiting for you.*
    </Markdown>
    <Markdown when="@wj1_why_top2.value.length &gt; 2">
*That's more than two — and Alma would say that's a good problem to have. Back in **Why I Write**, narrow it
to **just two**: which two really come up most?*
    </Markdown>

    <ActionButton id="wj1_generate_btn" label="Generate your writer profile"
                  when="@wj1_why_top2.value.length === 2">
      <SetFieldAction target="wj1_generate_flag" field="value" value="go" />
    </ActionButton>
    <Hidden>
      <LineInput id="wj1_generate_flag" />
    </Hidden>

    <IntakeGate ready="@wj1_generate_flag.value === 'go'">
      <Markdown> </Markdown>
      <Vertical id="wj1_type_result">

        <!-- 🤝 Community Writer  <- stay_in_touch, community -->
        <IntakeGate ready="'stay_in_touch' in @wj1_why_top2.value || 'community' in @wj1_why_top2.value">
          <Markdown> </Markdown>
          <Markdown>
| 🤝 The Community Writer | Core motivation: connection |
| :-- | :-- |
| **What it looks like** | You write to reach other people — messages, group chats, replies, shared docs — and you get sharper through back-and-forth with real readers. |
| **Why you're seeing it** | One of your two biggest reasons was *staying in touch* or *being part of a group.* |
| **A growth edge** | Take in feedback without becoming dependent on it. |
          </Markdown>
        </IntakeGate>

        <!-- 🧭 Explorer  <- figure_out, try_out -->
        <IntakeGate ready="'figure_out' in @wj1_why_top2.value || 'try_out' in @wj1_why_top2.value">
          <Markdown> </Markdown>
          <Markdown>
| 🧭 The Explorer | Core motivation: curiosity |
| :-- | :-- |
| **What it looks like** | You write to find out what you think — often not knowing where a piece is going until you're in it. Writing is a way of thinking, and you like to experiment. *(Lee's type.)* |
| **Why you're seeing it** | One of your two biggest reasons was *figuring out what you think* or *trying something out.* |
| **A growth edge** | Decide when exploring is the goal and when a piece needs to land somewhere. |
          </Markdown>
        </IntakeGate>

        <!-- 🔧 Technician  <- get_done, required -->
        <IntakeGate ready="'get_done' in @wj1_why_top2.value || 'required' in @wj1_why_top2.value">
          <Markdown> </Markdown>
          <Markdown>
| 🔧 The Technician | Core motivation: getting it done, and right |
| :-- | :-- |
| **What it looks like** | You write to accomplish a task cleanly — clear, organized, no wasted motion — and you like doing it properly the first time. *(Richard's type.)* |
| **Why you're seeing it** | One of your two biggest reasons was *getting something done / staying organized* or *because a class or job requires it.* |
| **A growth edge** | The parts of writing beyond correctness — voice, surprise, the reader's experience. |
          </Markdown>
        </IntakeGate>

        <!-- 🔁 Practitioner  <- remember -->
        <IntakeGate ready="'remember' in @wj1_why_top2.value">
          <Markdown> </Markdown>
          <Markdown>
| 🔁 The Practitioner | Core motivation: steady practice |
| :-- | :-- |
| **What it looks like** | You write regularly and usefully — to keep track, to remember, to stay on top of things. You trust the habit more than inspiration, and it adds up. |
| **Why you're seeing it** | One of your two biggest reasons was *to remember something.* |
| **A growth edge** | Let some of that steady practice become writing you do for its own sake. |
          </Markdown>
        </IntakeGate>

        <!-- 🏁 Finisher  <- proud -->
        <IntakeGate ready="'proud' in @wj1_why_top2.value">
          <Markdown> </Markdown>
          <Markdown>
| 🏁 The Finisher | Core motivation: finishing something real |
| :-- | :-- |
| **What it looks like** | You write to end up with a finished thing — something you can point to and say *I made that.* You have, or are building, the persistence to see a piece through. |
| **Why you're seeing it** | One of your two biggest reasons was *making something you're proud of.* |
| **A growth edge** | Treat finishing as the start of a writing life, not a one-time test. |
          </Markdown>
        </IntakeGate>

        <!-- 🌿 Hobbyist  <- enjoy -->
        <IntakeGate ready="'enjoy' in @wj1_why_top2.value">
          <Markdown> </Markdown>
          <Markdown>
| 🌿 The Hobbyist | Core motivation: personal fulfillment |
| :-- | :-- |
| **What it looks like** | You write because you *like* it. You don't necessarily need a big audience or a grade — the writing itself is the point, seen or not. |
| **Why you're seeing it** | One of your two biggest reasons was *because you enjoy it.* |
| **A growth edge** | Protect the joy of it even as you push to grow. |
          </Markdown>
        </IntakeGate>

        <!-- 💭 Dreamer  <- feelings -->
        <IntakeGate ready="'feelings' in @wj1_why_top2.value">
          <Markdown> </Markdown>
          <Markdown>
| 💭 The Dreamer | Core motivation: expression |
| :-- | :-- |
| **What it looks like** | You write to get what's inside into words — to work out how you feel and make sense of things. There's an inner life your writing reaches for, polished or not. |
| **Why you're seeing it** | One of your two biggest reasons was *to work out how you're feeling.* |
| **A growth edge** | Turn some of that inner material into pieces you finish and share. |
          </Markdown>
        </IntakeGate>

        <!-- 🎯 Pragmatist  <- persuade -->
        <IntakeGate ready="'persuade' in @wj1_why_top2.value">
          <Markdown> </Markdown>
          <Markdown>
| 🎯 The Pragmatist | Core motivation: audience &amp; impact |
| :-- | :-- |
| **What it looks like** | You write to *reach* people — to make a point, change a mind, get a result. You think about the reader on the other end, not just the words on the page. |
| **Why you're seeing it** | One of your two biggest reasons was *to persuade someone or make a point.* |
| **A growth edge** | Keep your own voice and purpose alongside the goal of landing with an audience. |
          </Markdown>
        </IntakeGate>

        <!-- fallback: no type-triggering reason selected -->
        <IntakeGate ready="!('stay_in_touch' in @wj1_why_top2.value || 'community' in @wj1_why_top2.value || 'figure_out' in @wj1_why_top2.value || 'try_out' in @wj1_why_top2.value || 'get_done' in @wj1_why_top2.value || 'required' in @wj1_why_top2.value || 'remember' in @wj1_why_top2.value || 'proud' in @wj1_why_top2.value || 'enjoy' in @wj1_why_top2.value || 'feelings' in @wj1_why_top2.value || 'persuade' in @wj1_why_top2.value)">
          <Markdown> </Markdown>
          <Markdown>*It looks like you haven't picked your **two biggest reasons** yet. Head back to **Why I Write**, tick your two, then come back and generate again.*</Markdown>
        </IntakeGate>

      </Vertical>
    </IntakeGate>

    <Markdown>
Once you've read it, think about how well it fits your actual writing practices and answer the below
questions.
    </Markdown>

    <!-- <Use>, not <Ref>: a Ref renders the STORED value, which is now a code
         like `figure_out`. <Use> re-renders the dropdown itself, so the student
         reads their own sentence back (and can nudge it, as on the board). -->
    <Collapsible id="wj1_type_lookback" title="Remind me what I said about why I write">
      <Markdown>**💬 Messaging &amp; staying in touch:**</Markdown>
      <Use ref="wj1_why_messaging" />
      <Markdown>**🎓 Coursework &amp; class writing:**</Markdown>
      <Use ref="wj1_why_coursework" />
      <Markdown>**🗂️ Notes, lists &amp; organizing:**</Markdown>
      <Use ref="wj1_why_organizing" />
      <Markdown>**📣 Posting &amp; sharing publicly:**</Markdown>
      <Use ref="wj1_why_posting" />
      <Markdown>**🎨 Creative &amp; personal writing:**</Markdown>
      <Use ref="wj1_why_creative" />
    </Collapsible>

    <Markdown>### Does this feel like you?</Markdown>
    <ChoiceInput id="wj1_fit">
      <Key value="yes"       > Yes — that's me </Key>
      <Key value="partly"    > Partly          </Key>
      <Key value="not_really"> Not really      </Key>
    </ChoiceInput>

    <Markdown>
### Your reflection

If you **agree** with your writer profile, think of and write a story from your writing life that
illustrates how this profile fits you.

If you **disagree** with your writer profile, think of a story from your writing life that illustrates why
this profile doesn't best capture your writing practices, and tell that story.

Your story should tell it what it couldn't know from your previous answers.
    </Markdown>
    <TextArea id="wj1_profile_reflect" rows="9" placeholder="Here's a story from my writing life..." />
  </Vertical>

  <!-- ===================================================================
       PRINT &amp; SUBMIT  —  THE SUBMISSION PAGE
       The one page that gets printed. It is mounted twice, and only ever one of
       those mounts is visible: as the "Print &amp; Submit" tab of
       writing_journal1_sba_part1.olx, and as the chat's final right-pane screen
       (`wj1_sidebar <- wj1_print`). Tabs keep every panel mounted but hide the
       inactive ones with display:none, which print inherits, so the PDF carries
       exactly one copy.

       Everything here must be MOUNTED at print time: window.print() photographs
       the live DOM, and a block React never rendered cannot be recovered by a
       print stylesheet. Three consequences, all load-bearing — do not "tidy"
       them away:
         * NO <Collapsible>. It renders children only when expanded, so a
           collapsed section exports as a bare heading.
         * NO <Tabs>, NO <UseHistory>. Both show one child at a time.
         * <IntakeGate> and when= ARE fine, and are used deliberately: they
           mount the branch the student earned, so the writer-type card prints
           as theirs alone.

       ORDER IS AN INVERTED TRIANGLE — what an instructor most needs to read
       comes first: the controls (print-hidden), then everything the student
       WROTE, then the structured answers, so the page can be scanned in a
       couple of minutes without paging.

       <Use> is a DAG reference, not a copy — the grids and the type card here
       are the SAME nodes as on the working screens, so they always agree.
       =================================================================== -->

  <Vertical id="wj1_print">

    <!-- The controls: on screen only. print="false" -> class print-hide, so the
         heading, the confirm box, the button and the how-to never reach the PDF. -->
    <Vertical id="wj1_print_controls" print="false">

      <Markdown>
## Print &amp; Submit

Here is your whole journal on one page, in the order your instructor will read it — your writing first.
Read it over; if you want to change an answer, go back to that step in the conversation, edit it, and come
back. Everything below updates to match.

When it looks right, tick the box and the **Export as PDF** button will appear. Save the file, then upload
it to your course system.
      </Markdown>

      <CheckboxInput id="wj1_completion_page">
        <Markdown>**Confirm that you are done with this journal entry.**</Markdown>
        <Key id="wj1_finished">I don't need to make any more changes.</Key>
      </CheckboxInput>

      <!-- Gated on the confirm box, matching WJ2. ActionButtons are hidden from
           print by print.css anyway; the wrapper above hides the rest. -->
      <ActionButton id="wj1_print_btn" label="Export as PDF"
                    when="'wj1_finished' in @wj1_completion_page.value">
        <PrintAction id="wj1_print_action" />
      </ActionButton>

      <Markdown when="'wj1_finished' in @wj1_completion_page.value">
## Journal Complete

In this journal you did the thing the assignment was really asking for: you stopped and **noticed the
writing you already do.** You built a **Writer Board** — for school and outside it — put the **why** on
it, saw the **writer type** your reasons point to, and then did the most important part: you said **what
your board shows about you as a writer.**

Hold onto Alma's point: a writer type is a **snapshot, not a verdict.** The writing you're about to do in
this course will keep changing the picture — which is exactly why it was worth mapping where you're
starting from.
      </Markdown>

    </Vertical>

